---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.js';
import { TIER_RULES, TIERS, TIER_BANDS, tierBandLabel, TIER_ORDER } from '../lib/rubric.js';

// This page renders the ACTUAL rules the build runs — TIER_RULES is imported
// from the same module the validator enforces, so this page cannot drift from
// the code. That is the point of it.
const title = `Methodology — how the verdicts are made · ${SITE.name}`;
const description =
  'The setup-tier rubric, verification semantics, and provenance rules behind every verdict on this site — the same rules the build enforces, rendered verbatim.';
---

<Base {title} {description}>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p>
      Every verdict on this site is mechanical. This page is the rulebook, rendered from the same
      code the build runs — if a badge feels wrong, the argument is with a rule below, not with a
      label someone typed.
    </p>

    <h2>The setup tier</h2>
    <p>
      Each project declares seven countable facts (<code>tierFactors</code>): how many containers
      it runs <em>excluding the shared reverse proxy</em>, whether it needs an external database,
      outbound mail, an OAuth registration, a DNS record, a GPU, and how many secrets the installer
      must generate. The tier is derived by the first matching rule, in order:
    </p>
    <ol class="rules">
      {
        TIER_RULES.map((r) => (
          <li>
            <p>
              <strong class={`tier-name tier-${r.tier}`}>{TIERS[r.tier]}</strong> if{' '}
              {r.test} (<code>{r.expr}</code>)
            </p>
            <p class="because">{r.because}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    <h2>Time bands</h2>
    <p>
      <code>timeToRunningMin</code> is the budget to set aside — through the first backup — not a
      stopwatch to the first screen. The build rejects a number outside its tier's band:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {
        TIER_ORDER.map((t) => (
          <li>
            <strong>{TIERS[t]}</strong>: {tierBandLabel(t)}
            {TIER_BANDS[t].maxMinutes === null ? '' : ` (${TIER_BANDS[t].minMinutes}–${TIER_BANDS[t].maxMinutes} min)`}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <h2>Two kinds of “verified”</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Tested by us</strong> means the install ran end-to-end on a clean, disposable server
      by our harness, and the stamp carries the date, version, and host. <strong>Reported working
      by readers</strong> is exactly that — self-reported, rate-limited, never faked, and never
      dressed up as more than it is. A page that fails a re-test is downgraded to
      {' '}<em>pending</em> at the next nightly build: out of the sitemap, visibly labelled, never
      silently stale. Nothing on this site can self-certify — verification state lives outside the
      contributable data on purpose.
    </p>
    <p>
      One limit worth stating plainly: <strong>the harness tests the server path</strong>. It
      creates a disposable machine, runs the install, asserts the health endpoint and the
      first-screen string, and destroys the box. The local prompts are written to the same standard
      — same pins, same acceptance criteria, same first backup — and reviewed against the same
      checklist, but no machine has run them end to end yet. A stamp on a page is a claim about the
      server path only, and the local path carries no stamp until a harness exists that can earn
      one.
    </p>

    <h2>Provenance</h2>
    <p>
      Every <code>compose.yml</code>, <code>compose.local.yml</code>, Caddy block, install script,
      and local prompt is authored by us from the upstream documentation, with the source URL
      recorded per artifact — never pasted from a repository. The local path is held to the same
      rule as the server one: the same pinned digests, authored from the same docs, checked by the
      same validator. Prices cite the vendor's own pricing page with the date we read it.
      awesome-selfhosted (CC-BY-SA) is a lead list, not a source. Drafts are AI-assisted; nothing
      earns a stamp a test didn't produce.
    </p>

    <h2>Credit</h2>
    <p>
      The question-shaped format is inspired by{' '}
      <a href={SITE.inspiredBy.url} rel="noopener">{SITE.inspiredBy.name}</a> — a different site by
      a different operator, asking a different question: theirs is whether AI can build you a
      replacement; ours is how you run the real thing.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: 68ch;
    padding-block: 3rem 4rem;
  }
  .prose h1 {
    margin-bottom: 0.5em;
  }
  .prose h2 {
    margin-top: 2em;
  }
  .rules {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }
  .rules .because {
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 0.25rem;
  }
  .tier-name {
    font-family: var(--font-mono, monospace);
    font-size: 0.85em;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  }
</style>
